A||||10||REDHOTT88||How to replace engine decklid shearpin?||||9||
Z||000000||REDHOTT88||12-07-2001||03:03 PM||||Hey all.. <P>I opened my decklid to check the oil, and the left pin that holds decklid down seems to have snapped in half. Not the hinged metal piece, but the small pin that sticks out and under the vents. Now the back left side near rear window sticks way up in air when you try to close.<P>Really sucks to open and close, and I want to fix it, but don't know how.(Nor where to find part. No junkyards have Fieros around here.)<P>Any help would be appreciated.<P>Thanks in advance, I know you guys will come thru!!!<P>------------------<BR>Steve<BR><B>1988 Formula 2.8L</B> V6 5 speed <BR><FONT COLOR="ffoooo"> (Red and shiny all over!)</FONT>||||reg||9
Z||000001||REDHOTT88||12-07-2001||05:37 PM||||I checked out the decklid last night when it was dark, and thought it was JUST a pin,   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_drunk.gif">   but the entire left side hinge assembly is what should be replaced. <BR>Doesn't look like the pin can be replaced. <P>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif">I'd still like some advice... anybody got a trick to remove and replace without insult or injury?<P>Anybody got one for sale?<P> Thanks<BR><p>[This message has been edited by REDHOTT88 (edited 12-07-2001).]||||reg||11
Z||000002||88-DOHC||12-07-2001||06:32 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by REDHOTT88:<BR><B>I checked out the decklid last night when it was dark, and thought it was JUST a pin,   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_drunk.gif">   but the entire left side hinge assembly that should be replaced. <BR>Doesn't look like the pin can be replaced. <P>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif">I'd still like some advice... anybody got a trick to remove and replace without insult or injury?<P>Anybody got one for sale?<P> Thanks<P>[This message has been edited by REDHOTT88 (edited 12-07-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Well, you will need to disconnect the decklid truss rod on that side before replacing the hinge.  Yes, there is easier and harder ways of doing this, but its rather hard to explain what to do.  My only advice is be very careful, as the torsion rods can easily snap up and crack your rear window. Once you get the decklid off, place a small peice of a 2x4 between the torsion rod and the window.  The only other thing is you will need a set of what are called "secure torx". Basicly torx with a hole in the tip.  Also having a 3-4 length of pipe that will fit over the end of the torsion rod is in order.  Again, this is all very hard to explain in words only. Once you get the torsion rod removed from the hinge, replacing the hinge is relatively easy although you might need another person with said pipe pull the torsion bar away from the window so you can get the hinge mounted down. Getting the torsion rod back on isn't to bad either, but is easiest with 2 people. One person with a BIG screwdriver, or pry bar, moving and holding the torsion rod into position while the second puts in the secure torx. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||REDHOTT88||12-07-2001||06:43 PM||||Thanks... I know it was a hard answer to put into words, but it's nice to know about the possibilty of a broken window!!<P>What exactly am I looking for as far as the "torsion rod" goes. I haven't really inspected it that well as of yet..||||reg||5
Z||000004||Bazooka||12-07-2001||07:07 PM||||From a previous post on Torsion Rod Replacement      Torsion springs are all color coded with some paint sprayed near the pivot point. When I added a wing to my GT, I put on torsion springs from the junkyard for $10. The spring was color coded 'WHITE' which says that it was designed for cars with the rear wing. The springs with red or blue are designed for less weight so I would avoid those. Also, all torsion springs have three adjustment holes for locating a pin. Pin is removed and relocated to another hole only by releasing the tension on the torsion spring (rods) either with a long lever placed under the hinge area or a thick rope to pull the rod link downward about 1 1/2 inches. See 'Articles' section from the NIFE website at <A HREF="http://www.xnet.com/~nife" TARGET=_blank>http://www.xnet.com/~nife</A>  entitled 'Tosion Rod Replacement'. A very complete set of directions are included here.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||REDHOTT88||12-07-2001||07:10 PM||||Awesome, Thanks Bazooka!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||artherd||12-07-2001||07:43 PM||||Don't replace the torsion rods, get Rodney's $69 strut kit.  Works great, looks better!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000007||REDHOTT88||12-07-2001||08:08 PM||||Yeah, but does it take place of hinge..I don't think so. <P>I don't want to replace the torsion rod, just seems to be part of hinge replacement, have to know how to handle that wild thing!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||Rodney||12-07-2001||08:09 PM||||The only other thing is you will need a set of what are called "secure torx". Basicly torx with a hole in the tip. <P>You can do it without the secure torex tool. Just take a small drift or something and hit the small pin in the middle and it will break off allowing you to use s standard torex driver on it. ||||reg||1
